1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,686
Sweetheart, there's one Christmas cookie that every

2
00:00:03,686 --> 00:00:06,289
grandmother in America used to make,

3
00:00:06,289 --> 00:00:08,602
and almost nobody makes anymore.

4
00:00:08,782 --> 00:00:11,620
Soft, chewy molasses cookies,

5
00:00:11,620 --> 00:00:13,087
dark and spicy,

6
00:00:13,087 --> 00:00:15,435
with crackly sugar tops.

7
00:00:15,615 --> 00:00:18,005
They make the whole house smell like

8
00:00:18,005 --> 00:00:18,668
Christmas.

9
00:00:18,848 --> 00:00:21,243
But when people try them today,

10
00:00:21,243 --> 00:00:23,561
they come out flat and greasy,

11
00:00:23,561 --> 00:00:25,878
or hard as hockey pucks by the

12
00:00:25,878 --> 00:00:26,883
next morning.

13
00:00:27,063 --> 00:00:30,313
And it's always the same few mistakes.

14
00:00:30,663 --> 00:00:33,907
So tonight I'm giving you Grandma Pearl's

15
00:00:33,907 --> 00:00:35,648
soft molasses cookies,

16
00:00:35,648 --> 00:00:36,676
step by step,

17
00:00:36,676 --> 00:00:39,603
and the five mistakes that ruin them.

18
00:00:39,783 --> 00:00:41,024
And stay with me,

19
00:00:41,024 --> 00:00:43,945
because the last secret is something she

20
00:00:43,945 --> 00:00:45,552
put in the cookie tin,

21
00:00:45,552 --> 00:00:47,742
and it keeps them soft for two

22
00:00:47,742 --> 00:00:48,618
whole weeks.

23
00:00:48,798 --> 00:00:51,432
People think I bake them fresh every

24
00:00:51,432 --> 00:00:51,724
day.

25
00:00:52,075 --> 00:00:54,944
Grandma Pearl kept her molasses cookies in

26
00:00:54,944 --> 00:00:56,993
a big round tin with a picture

27
00:00:56,993 --> 00:00:58,564
of a sleigh on the lid.

28
00:00:58,744 --> 00:01:00,813
It sat on top of her icebox

29
00:01:00,813 --> 00:01:03,264
from Thanksgiving to New Year's,

30
00:01:03,264 --> 00:01:05,025
and it was never empty.

31
00:01:05,205 --> 00:01:07,403
Danny and I would climb on a

32
00:01:07,403 --> 00:01:08,816
chair to reach it.

33
00:01:08,996 --> 00:01:10,448
She always knew,

34
00:01:10,448 --> 00:01:10,993
honey.

35
00:01:11,173 --> 00:01:12,220
She'd say, two,

36
00:01:12,220 --> 00:01:14,385
not three, from the other room,

37
00:01:14,385 --> 00:01:16,340
without even turning around.

38
00:01:16,690 --> 00:01:19,862
Molasses was the sweetener of poor families

39
00:01:19,862 --> 00:01:22,297
for two hundred years in America.

40
00:01:22,477 --> 00:01:24,598
White sugar was expensive,

41
00:01:24,598 --> 00:01:26,475
and molasses was cheap,

42
00:01:26,475 --> 00:01:28,514
so it went in everything.

43
00:01:28,694 --> 00:01:31,011
Baked beans, brown bread,

44
00:01:31,011 --> 00:01:33,884
gingerbread, and these cookies.

45
00:01:34,064 --> 00:01:36,121
In a minute I'll tell you which

46
00:01:36,121 --> 00:01:37,713
kind of molasses to buy,

47
00:01:37,713 --> 00:01:40,368
because one kind will ruin your cookies,

48
00:01:40,368 --> 00:01:42,624
and it's sitting right next to the

49
00:01:42,624 --> 00:01:44,083
good one on the shelf.

50
00:01:44,434 --> 00:01:47,418
Grandma Pearl made these every December for

51
00:01:47,418 --> 00:01:49,361
the men who came to her farm

52
00:01:49,361 --> 00:01:51,027
to help butcher the hog,

53
00:01:51,027 --> 00:01:52,415
and for the mailman,

54
00:01:52,415 --> 00:01:53,872
and for the minister.

55
00:01:54,052 --> 00:01:56,337
She packed a dozen in waxed paper

56
00:01:56,337 --> 00:01:57,860
for every one of them.

57
00:01:58,040 --> 00:02:00,617
She said, a molasses cookie in your

58
00:02:00,617 --> 00:02:03,121
coat pocket will keep you warm all

59
00:02:03,121 --> 00:02:04,078
the way home.

60
00:02:04,258 --> 00:02:05,837
She wasn't wrong,

61
00:02:05,837 --> 00:02:06,394
honey.

62
00:02:06,574 --> 00:02:08,373
They're full of ginger.

63
00:02:08,724 --> 00:02:11,057
And the first time I made them

64
00:02:11,057 --> 00:02:12,379
by myself, honey,

65
00:02:12,379 --> 00:02:13,390
I was twelve,

66
00:02:13,390 --> 00:02:15,957
and I used a tablespoon of ground

67
00:02:15,957 --> 00:02:18,602
cloves instead of a half teaspoon,

68
00:02:18,602 --> 00:02:22,179
because I misread Grandma Pearl's handwriting.

69
00:02:22,359 --> 00:02:25,041
My father took one bite and said,

70
00:02:25,041 --> 00:02:27,561
Ruthie, my whole mouth is numb.

71
00:02:27,741 --> 00:02:30,874
Danny said they tasted like the dentist's

72
00:02:30,874 --> 00:02:31,410
office.

73
00:02:31,590 --> 00:02:34,385
Grandma Pearl laughed until she cried,

74
00:02:34,385 --> 00:02:37,034
and then she rewrote the recipe card

75
00:02:37,034 --> 00:02:38,579
in big block letters.

76
00:02:38,929 --> 00:02:41,387
Here's what you need for about three

77
00:02:41,387 --> 00:02:41,796
dozen.

78
00:02:41,976 --> 00:02:44,942
Three quarters of a cup of butter,

79
00:02:44,942 --> 00:02:45,378
soft.

80
00:02:45,558 --> 00:02:47,748
One cup of brown sugar,

81
00:02:47,748 --> 00:02:48,414
packed.

82
00:02:48,594 --> 00:02:49,662
One egg.

83
00:02:49,842 --> 00:02:52,501
One quarter cup of molasses.

84
00:02:52,681 --> 00:02:55,038
Two and a quarter cups of flour.

85
00:02:55,218 --> 00:02:57,725
Two teaspoons of baking soda.

86
00:02:57,905 --> 00:03:00,123
A quarter teaspoon of salt.

87
00:03:00,303 --> 00:03:02,173
Two teaspoons of ginger,

88
00:03:02,173 --> 00:03:03,420
one of cinnamon,

89
00:03:03,420 --> 00:03:05,446
half a teaspoon of cloves.

90
00:03:05,626 --> 00:03:07,779
And a little bowl of white sugar

91
00:03:07,779 --> 00:03:08,587
for rolling.

92
00:03:08,937 --> 00:03:11,142
And here's mistake number one.

93
00:03:11,322 --> 00:03:13,691
Using blackstrap molasses.

94
00:03:13,871 --> 00:03:16,623
Blackstrap is the last boiling of the

95
00:03:16,623 --> 00:03:19,449
sugar cane, and it's bitter and harsh,

96
00:03:19,449 --> 00:03:21,011
almost like medicine.

97
00:03:21,191 --> 00:03:23,676
It makes your cookies taste burnt.

98
00:03:23,856 --> 00:03:26,369
You want the one that says original

99
00:03:26,369 --> 00:03:26,944
or mild.

100
00:03:27,124 --> 00:03:29,912
It's sweet and warm and tastes like

101
00:03:29,912 --> 00:03:30,549
caramel.

102
00:03:30,729 --> 00:03:34,200
Grandma Pearl called blackstrap the cough syrup,

103
00:03:34,200 --> 00:03:36,731
and wouldn't let it in her kitchen.

104
00:03:37,080 --> 00:03:39,124
And mistake number two.

105
00:03:39,304 --> 00:03:41,266
Using old spices.

106
00:03:41,446 --> 00:03:44,311
Ground ginger and cinnamon lose their flavor

107
00:03:44,311 --> 00:03:46,589
after about a year in the cupboard.

108
00:03:46,769 --> 00:03:49,464
If yours has been sitting there since

109
00:03:49,464 --> 00:03:50,410
you moved in,

110
00:03:50,410 --> 00:03:51,576
buy a fresh jar.

111
00:03:51,756 --> 00:03:53,625
Open it and smell it.

112
00:03:53,805 --> 00:03:55,748
If it doesn't make you sneeze a

113
00:03:55,748 --> 00:03:58,256
little, it won't make your cookies taste

114
00:03:58,256 --> 00:03:59,134
like anything.

115
00:03:59,314 --> 00:04:02,344
A fresh jar costs about two dollars.

116
00:04:02,694 --> 00:04:05,201
Beat the soft butter and brown sugar

117
00:04:05,201 --> 00:04:07,988
together until they're light and fluffy,

118
00:04:07,988 --> 00:04:09,242
about two minutes.

119
00:04:09,422 --> 00:04:12,208
Beat in the egg and the molasses.

120
00:04:12,388 --> 00:04:13,908
In another bowl,

121
00:04:13,908 --> 00:04:15,427
whisk the flour,

122
00:04:15,427 --> 00:04:17,137
baking soda, salt,

123
00:04:17,137 --> 00:04:18,181
and spices.

124
00:04:18,361 --> 00:04:20,795
Then stir the dry into the wet,

125
00:04:20,795 --> 00:04:23,621
just until there's no flour showing.

126
00:04:23,801 --> 00:04:26,207
The dough will be soft and sticky,

127
00:04:26,207 --> 00:04:28,897
honey, the color of coffee with cream.

128
00:04:29,077 --> 00:04:30,064
That's right.

129
00:04:30,415 --> 00:04:32,227
If this is helping you,

130
00:04:32,227 --> 00:04:34,354
tap the like button for me,

131
00:04:34,354 --> 00:04:35,221
sweetheart.

132
00:04:35,401 --> 00:04:37,978
It helps Grandma more than you know.

133
00:04:38,158 --> 00:04:40,666
And here's mistake number three.

134
00:04:40,846 --> 00:04:42,878
Baking the dough right away.

135
00:04:43,058 --> 00:04:44,290
It's too soft,

136
00:04:44,290 --> 00:04:47,194
and the cookies spread into thin,

137
00:04:47,194 --> 00:04:48,515
greasy puddles.

138
00:04:48,695 --> 00:04:50,988
Cover the bowl and chill it for

139
00:04:50,988 --> 00:04:52,319
at least one hour,

140
00:04:52,319 --> 00:04:53,281
or overnight.

141
00:04:53,461 --> 00:04:55,976
Cold dough holds its shape and bakes

142
00:04:55,976 --> 00:04:57,303
up thick and chewy.

143
00:04:57,654 --> 00:05:00,463
Grandma Pearl made her dough the night

144
00:05:00,463 --> 00:05:02,680
before and left it on the cold

145
00:05:02,680 --> 00:05:03,493
back porch.

146
00:05:03,673 --> 00:05:04,917
In the morning,

147
00:05:04,917 --> 00:05:06,576
it was firm as clay.

148
00:05:06,756 --> 00:05:09,102
And the spices had all night to

149
00:05:09,102 --> 00:05:10,919
blend into the molasses,

150
00:05:10,919 --> 00:05:12,584
so they tasted richer.

151
00:05:12,764 --> 00:05:14,602
If you make the dough on a

152
00:05:14,602 --> 00:05:16,723
Friday and bake on a Saturday,

153
00:05:16,723 --> 00:05:19,409
you'll have better cookies than if you

154
00:05:19,409 --> 00:05:20,752
did it all at once.

155
00:05:21,101 --> 00:05:23,866
And measure your flour the right way,

156
00:05:23,866 --> 00:05:26,929
sweetheart, because too much flour is the

157
00:05:26,929 --> 00:05:29,170
other way to get hard cookies.

158
00:05:29,350 --> 00:05:31,885
Don't scoop the cup into the flour

159
00:05:31,885 --> 00:05:32,183
bag.

160
00:05:32,363 --> 00:05:34,281
That packs it down and you get

161
00:05:34,281 --> 00:05:36,775
a quarter cup extra without knowing it.

162
00:05:36,955 --> 00:05:39,008
Spoon the flour into the cup,

163
00:05:39,008 --> 00:05:41,203
then level it off with the back

164
00:05:41,203 --> 00:05:41,982
of a knife.

165
00:05:42,162 --> 00:05:45,360
It's the single most common baking mistake

166
00:05:45,360 --> 00:05:47,721
in America, and it's so easy to

167
00:05:47,721 --> 00:05:48,025
fix.

168
00:05:48,376 --> 00:05:50,860
Heat your oven to three hundred fifty.

169
00:05:51,040 --> 00:05:53,390
Scoop the dough into balls about the

170
00:05:53,390 --> 00:05:54,500
size of a walnut.

171
00:05:54,680 --> 00:05:57,082
Roll each one in white sugar until

172
00:05:57,082 --> 00:05:58,778
it's completely covered.

173
00:05:58,958 --> 00:06:01,313
Put them on a baking sheet lined

174
00:06:01,313 --> 00:06:03,741
with parchment, two inches apart.

175
00:06:03,921 --> 00:06:05,582
They'll spread a little.

176
00:06:05,762 --> 00:06:07,090
And as they bake,

177
00:06:07,090 --> 00:06:09,746
the sugar on top cracks into those

178
00:06:09,746 --> 00:06:13,262
beautiful little crinkles that make them look

179
00:06:13,262 --> 00:06:14,434
like Christmas.

180
00:06:14,784 --> 00:06:17,083
Bake for nine to ten minutes.

181
00:06:17,263 --> 00:06:19,783
And here's mistake number four,

182
00:06:19,783 --> 00:06:21,652
the most important one.

183
00:06:21,832 --> 00:06:24,305
Baking them until they look done.

184
00:06:24,485 --> 00:06:26,210
When you take them out,

185
00:06:26,210 --> 00:06:28,010
the edges should be set,

186
00:06:28,010 --> 00:06:30,785
but the centers should look puffy and

187
00:06:30,785 --> 00:06:32,285
a little underbaked,

188
00:06:32,285 --> 00:06:32,960
even raw.

189
00:06:33,140 --> 00:06:34,893
Take them out anyway.

190
00:06:35,073 --> 00:06:37,540
They keep baking on the hot pan,

191
00:06:37,540 --> 00:06:39,544
and they settle into soft,

192
00:06:39,544 --> 00:06:40,623
chewy middles.

193
00:06:40,803 --> 00:06:42,079
One extra minute,

194
00:06:42,079 --> 00:06:44,181
and you've got hockey pucks.

195
00:06:44,531 --> 00:06:46,452
Let them sit on the hot pan

196
00:06:46,452 --> 00:06:49,155
for five minutes before you move them,

197
00:06:49,155 --> 00:06:49,582
honey.

198
00:06:49,762 --> 00:06:52,199
They're too soft to lift at first,

199
00:06:52,199 --> 00:06:53,489
and they'll break.

200
00:06:53,669 --> 00:06:55,695
Then move them to a rack to

201
00:06:55,695 --> 00:06:56,896
cool completely.

202
00:06:57,076 --> 00:06:59,204
And if you want a little extra

203
00:06:59,204 --> 00:07:02,396
Christmas, press a few pieces of crystallized

204
00:07:02,396 --> 00:07:04,878
ginger into the tops before baking.

205
00:07:05,058 --> 00:07:08,167
My granddaughter likes them dipped halfway in

206
00:07:08,167 --> 00:07:09,273
white chocolate.

207
00:07:09,622 --> 00:07:11,511
Now mistake number five,

208
00:07:11,511 --> 00:07:13,872
and the secret I promised you.

209
00:07:14,052 --> 00:07:15,387
Storing them wrong.

210
00:07:15,567 --> 00:07:18,584
Molasses cookies dry out fast in a

211
00:07:18,584 --> 00:07:20,803
cookie jar or on a plate.

212
00:07:20,983 --> 00:07:23,333
Grandma Pearl kept them in a tin

213
00:07:23,333 --> 00:07:24,582
with a tight lid.

214
00:07:24,762 --> 00:07:26,212
And in that tin,

215
00:07:26,212 --> 00:07:29,201
she always put one slice of plain

216
00:07:29,201 --> 00:07:30,288
white bread.

217
00:07:30,468 --> 00:07:31,852
Just one slice,

218
00:07:31,852 --> 00:07:33,882
tucked in at the side.

219
00:07:34,062 --> 00:07:36,558
And they stayed soft for two weeks.

220
00:07:36,907 --> 00:07:39,152
The cookies pull the moisture out of

221
00:07:39,152 --> 00:07:39,775
the bread.

222
00:07:39,955 --> 00:07:41,580
A couple of days later,

223
00:07:41,580 --> 00:07:43,770
the bread is hard as a cracker,

224
00:07:43,770 --> 00:07:45,889
and the cookies are as soft as

225
00:07:45,889 --> 00:07:47,444
the day you made them.

226
00:07:47,624 --> 00:07:49,662
When the bread gets hard,

227
00:07:49,662 --> 00:07:51,374
put in a fresh slice.

228
00:07:51,554 --> 00:07:54,129
It works for any soft cookie,

229
00:07:54,129 --> 00:07:55,106
sweetheart.

230
00:07:55,286 --> 00:07:58,244
Sugar cookies, oatmeal cookies,

231
00:07:58,244 --> 00:07:59,675
snickerdoodles.

232
00:07:59,855 --> 00:08:02,946
It's the oldest trick in any grandmother's

233
00:08:02,946 --> 00:08:03,535
kitchen.

234
00:08:03,885 --> 00:08:05,848
And they freeze beautifully.

235
00:08:06,028 --> 00:08:07,859
Freeze the dough balls,

236
00:08:07,859 --> 00:08:09,769
already rolled in sugar,

237
00:08:09,769 --> 00:08:10,565
on a tray,

238
00:08:10,565 --> 00:08:12,715
and then put them in a bag.

239
00:08:12,895 --> 00:08:15,443
Bake them straight from the freezer,

240
00:08:15,443 --> 00:08:16,575
adding a minute.

241
00:08:16,755 --> 00:08:18,868
I keep a bag in my freezer

242
00:08:18,868 --> 00:08:19,925
all December.

243
00:08:20,105 --> 00:08:21,867
When company drops by,

244
00:08:21,867 --> 00:08:22,748
I bake six,

245
00:08:22,748 --> 00:08:26,033
and the house smells like Grandma Pearl's

246
00:08:26,033 --> 00:08:28,116
kitchen in eleven minutes.

247
00:08:28,465 --> 00:08:30,335
And if they do go hard,

248
00:08:30,335 --> 00:08:32,041
don't throw them out,

249
00:08:32,041 --> 00:08:32,529
honey.

250
00:08:32,709 --> 00:08:35,254
Crush them into crumbs with a rolling

251
00:08:35,254 --> 00:08:37,111
pin and use them as a crust

252
00:08:37,111 --> 00:08:39,449
for a pumpkin pie or a cheesecake,

253
00:08:39,449 --> 00:08:41,788
mixed with a little melted butter.

254
00:08:41,968 --> 00:08:44,731
Or crumble them over vanilla ice cream.

255
00:08:44,911 --> 00:08:48,499
Grandma Pearl never threw away one crumb.

256
00:08:48,679 --> 00:08:50,817
She said a stale cookie is just

257
00:08:50,817 --> 00:08:53,024
a pie crust that doesn't know it

258
00:08:53,024 --> 00:08:53,299
yet.

259
00:08:53,649 --> 00:08:55,825
These are the best cookie in the

260
00:08:55,825 --> 00:08:56,980
world for giving,

261
00:08:56,980 --> 00:08:57,388
honey.

262
00:08:57,568 --> 00:08:58,868
They travel well,

263
00:08:58,868 --> 00:09:00,167
they don't break,

264
00:09:00,167 --> 00:09:01,620
and they stay soft.

265
00:09:01,800 --> 00:09:04,301
Stack six in a tall glass jar,

266
00:09:04,301 --> 00:09:06,719
or wrap them in wax paper and

267
00:09:06,719 --> 00:09:08,804
tie them with red string,

268
00:09:08,804 --> 00:09:10,972
the way Grandma Pearl did.

269
00:09:11,152 --> 00:09:13,352
Put them in the mailbox for the

270
00:09:13,352 --> 00:09:14,275
mail carrier.

271
00:09:14,455 --> 00:09:16,170
Take a plate to the nurses at

272
00:09:16,170 --> 00:09:16,939
the hospital.

273
00:09:17,119 --> 00:09:19,906
They cost about eight cents a cookie.

274
00:09:20,255 --> 00:09:22,734
Every Christmas, I bring a tin to

275
00:09:22,734 --> 00:09:25,213
the fire station down the street.

276
00:09:25,393 --> 00:09:27,561
The young men there call me the

277
00:09:27,561 --> 00:09:28,400
cookie lady.

278
00:09:28,580 --> 00:09:30,951
Last year, one of them told me

279
00:09:30,951 --> 00:09:34,508
his grandmother used to make molasses cookies

280
00:09:34,508 --> 00:09:36,009
exactly like these,

281
00:09:36,009 --> 00:09:38,460
and he hadn't had one since she

282
00:09:38,460 --> 00:09:38,855
died.

283
00:09:39,035 --> 00:09:41,495
He ate four standing right there,

284
00:09:41,495 --> 00:09:43,731
and then he went outside for a

285
00:09:43,731 --> 00:09:44,178
while.

286
00:09:44,358 --> 00:09:46,668
I understood, sweetheart.

287
00:09:47,018 --> 00:09:48,563
So one more time.

288
00:09:48,743 --> 00:09:51,622
Mild molasses, never blackstrap.

289
00:09:51,802 --> 00:09:53,300
Fresh spices.

290
00:09:53,480 --> 00:09:55,837
Chill the dough at least an hour,

291
00:09:55,837 --> 00:09:56,765
or overnight.

292
00:09:56,945 --> 00:09:58,571
Roll them in sugar.

293
00:09:58,751 --> 00:10:00,775
Take them out when the middles still

294
00:10:00,775 --> 00:10:01,618
look underdone.

295
00:10:01,798 --> 00:10:04,023
And a slice of bread in the

296
00:10:04,023 --> 00:10:04,352
tin.

297
00:10:04,532 --> 00:10:07,610
That's Grandma Pearl's molasses cookie,

298
00:10:07,610 --> 00:10:09,109
soft for two weeks,

299
00:10:09,109 --> 00:10:11,870
and the smell of Christmas in every

300
00:10:11,870 --> 00:10:12,265
bite.

301
00:10:12,614 --> 00:10:14,350
Tell me in the comments,

302
00:10:14,350 --> 00:10:15,145
sweetheart.

303
00:10:15,325 --> 00:10:18,576
Did your grandmother make molasses cookies?

304
00:10:18,756 --> 00:10:20,838
And did she let you have two,

305
00:10:20,838 --> 00:10:21,485
or three?

306
00:10:21,665 --> 00:10:24,761
The whole playlist of Grandma's recipes is

307
00:10:24,761 --> 00:10:26,751
right there on your screen,

308
00:10:26,751 --> 00:10:28,815
and I'll see you in the next

309
00:10:28,815 --> 00:10:29,258
video.

310
00:10:29,438 --> 00:10:31,016
Good night, honey.

311
00:10:31,196 --> 00:10:32,729
Merry Christmas.
